(ENV-11: the "Complete Example" (LANGUAGE.md:4609-4627), adapted --
 the greeting/precedence half only, with a namespaced env-var name
 instead of `GREET_NAME` (so no real host accidentally sets it) and the
 trailing `environment's "USER"` print DROPPED rather than reproduced,
 on the same principle `gen_misc.vox:39-101` already establishes for
 this project: a probe must never print raw host environment state,
 since one already leaked into a checked-in fixture. The USER lookup
 itself is exactly ENV-06/ENV-07's construct and doesn't need
 re-proving here. Checked block is the default run (no arg, no
 variable, namespaced so no host sets it by accident); the other two
 precedence conditions are the second, unchecked block, hand-verified
 once each.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox ENV-11.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   Hello,
   World
   !
)
(Second and third runs, hand-verified, NOT checked by check-probes.sh:
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox ENV-11.vox -o p && VF_PROBE_GREET_NAME=Zoe ./p
 output: Hello, / Zoe / !  (env var used since no argument passed)
 Ran: ... && VF_PROBE_GREET_NAME=Zoe ./p Bob
 output: Hello, / Bob / !  (argument beats env var, exactly as documented)
)
a text called name is "World".

If arguments's count is greater than 1 then,
    the name is arguments's first.
But if the environment variable "VF_PROBE_GREET_NAME" exists then,
    the name is environment's "VF_PROBE_GREET_NAME".

Print "Hello, ".
Print the name.
Print "!".
